From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 01:21:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(control): a triple click highlights the whole line

Cascadia highlights the full width of the line and trims on copy. ghostty's
default stops where the line's content stops, so side by side in one window the
ghostty pane's triple click visibly falls short - and the highlight is drawn
from ghostty's own selection extent, so nothing on this side could fix it.

selection-trim-line = false asks for the wider extent. What gets copied is
trimmed either way, which is the half that had to be checked separately rather
than assumed: scripts/smoke-harness.ps1 triple clicks a 26-character line in a
71-column terminal and requires the selection to grow to 70 cells while the
text stays 26 characters.

WT suites: terminalCore 54/54, unitSettingsModel 159/159, unitControl 55/55,
terminalApp 51/51.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BgDGqEqRBB8L5Ueoq1uuxy
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp  |  8 ++++++++
 .../UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp     | 14 ++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
index b7e9001bd..ce1b9199f 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
@@ -98,6 +98,14 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             }
         }
 
+        // A triple click highlights the whole width of the line here, the way
+        // cascadia's does. ghostty's own default stops where the line's content
+        // stops, which reads as a highlight that falls short next to a cascadia
+        // pane in the same window. What gets copied is trimmed either way, so
+        // this widens the highlight without putting trailing spaces on the
+        // clipboard - measured, because that was the regression to fear.
+        set("selection-trim-line", "false");
+
         // --- Padding ------------------------------------------------------
         // WT already insets the SwapChainPanel by this same padding, so asking
         // ghostty to pad as well would double it. Ours is therefore zero, and
diff --git a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
index 3d57ca2d4..d148aafa9 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
@@ -39,6 +39,7 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         TEST_METHOD(PaddingIsZeroBecauseWindowsTerminalAlreadyPads);
         TEST_METHOD(SchemeProducesForegroundBackgroundAndFullPalette);
         TEST_METHOD(WordDelimitersReachTheEngine);
+        TEST_METHOD(TripleClickHighlightsTheWholeLine);
         TEST_METHOD(BackslashesAreDoubledForTheWordCharsFieldOnly);
         TEST_METHOD(NullSettingsProduceNothing);
 
@@ -169,6 +170,19 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
                        L"ghostty's word boundaries must come from WT's setting");
     }
 
+    // ghostty's triple click stops where the line's content stops; cascadia's
+    // runs to the edge of the pane. Side by side in one window the difference
+    // is obvious, and it is drawn from ghostty's selection extent, so it can
+    // only be fixed by asking for the wider one.
+    void GhosttySettingsTests::TripleClickHighlightsTheWholeLine()
+    {
+        const auto entries = Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(
+            *_settings());
+
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(entries, "selection-trim-line", "false"),
+                       L"a triple click must highlight the full width of the line");
+    }
+
     // The one escaping rule left, and it belongs to the field rather than to
     // the entry. ghostty parses selection-word-chars as a Zig string literal,
     // so a lone backslash starts an escape sequence there - and WT's default
